From 9e6a2eadada1cbdb18ab8efa483b86920195a5dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ward Ramsdell <ward@rds-or.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:29:28 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] net: gptp: Do not count stale Pdelay responses toward
 as_capable check

gptp_handle_msg() increments pdelay_req_state->rcvd_pdelay_resp and
rcvd_pdelay_follow_up unconditionally for every received
PATH_DELAY_RESP_MESSAGE / PATH_DELAY_FOLLOWUP_MESSAGE, before any
sequence-id validation runs. gptp_handle_pdelay_resp() and
gptp_handle_pdelay_follow_up() do validate the sequence id later, but
only against the single stored rcvd_pdelay_resp_ptr / rcvd_pdelay_
follow_up_ptr - the counters themselves have already been incremented
for every message of that type received during the current Pdelay_Req
cycle, valid or not.

gptp_md_pdelay_req_state_machine() uses those same counters to decide
whether "multiple responses" were received for one request, and if so
clears port_ds->as_capable - which gates all downstream Sync processing
and clock correction (gptp_mi_pss_rcv_state_machine() discards
everything while as_capable is false).

A late or duplicate response left over from an earlier Pdelay_Req cycle
- ordinary network jitter or a marginal link, not necessarily
misbehavior by the peer - can arrive after a new request has already
been sent. It gets rejected by the later sequence-id check and does not
replace the stored pointer, but it has already incremented the counter.
When the real, matching response for the current cycle arrives next,
the counter reaches 2, incorrectly triggering "too many responses" and
clearing as_capable even though only one of the two messages was ever a
valid match for the outstanding request.

Observed on real hardware (two independent boards running gPTP over a
direct Ethernet link): as_capable was repeatedly and persistently
cleared this way whenever the link had any jitter, which in turn
prevented the frequency/offset servo (gptp_update_local_port_clock())
from ever running continuously, and made recovery from a real transient
clock disturbance (a large step change in one board's oscillator
frequency) fail outright, since the port could not stay AS-capable long
enough for Sync-derived corrections to catch up.

Fix by discarding a response or follow-up whose sequence id does not
match the currently outstanding request before incrementing either
counter, so only genuine duplicates/multiple-responses for the same
request are counted, not late arrivals from a previous cycle.

Signed-off-by: Ward Ramsdell <ward@rds-or.com>
---
 subsys/net/l2/ethernet/gptp/gptp.c | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+)

diff --git a/subsys/net/l2/ethernet/gptp/gptp.c b/subsys/net/l2/ethernet/gptp/gptp.c
index 1a43f8dbf3d..8f48398faa1 100644
--- a/subsys/net/l2/ethernet/gptp/gptp.c
+++ b/subsys/net/l2/ethernet/gptp/gptp.c
@@ -203,6 +203,24 @@ static void gptp_handle_msg(struct net_pkt *pkt)
 
 		PRINT_INFO("PDELAY_RESP", hdr, pkt);
 
+		/* Only count this response toward the "multiple responses"
+		 * AS-capable check (gptp_md_pdelay_req_state_machine()) if
+		 * its sequence id matches the currently outstanding request.
+		 * Without this, a late/stale response left over from an
+		 * earlier Pdelay_Req cycle - ordinary network jitter, not
+		 * actual peer misbehavior - still increments
+		 * rcvd_pdelay_resp here, before gptp_handle_pdelay_resp()'s
+		 * own sequence-id check ever runs, silently inflating the
+		 * counter that decides "too many responses" and clears
+		 * as_capable even though only one of the two was ever a real
+		 * match for the current request.
+		 */
+		if (!pdelay_req_state->tx_pdelay_req_ptr ||
+		    GPTP_HDR(pdelay_req_state->tx_pdelay_req_ptr)->sequence_id !=
+			    hdr->sequence_id) {
+			break;
+		}
+
 		pdelay_req_state->rcvd_pdelay_resp++;
 
 		/* If we already have one, drop the received one. */
@@ -254,6 +272,16 @@ static void gptp_handle_msg(struct net_pkt *pkt)
 
 		PRINT_INFO("PDELAY_FOLLOWUP", hdr, pkt);
 
+		/* Same reasoning as the PDELAY_RESP case above - don't let a
+		 * stale follow-up left over from an earlier cycle inflate the
+		 * "multiple responses" counter.
+		 */
+		if (!pdelay_req_state->tx_pdelay_req_ptr ||
+		    GPTP_HDR(pdelay_req_state->tx_pdelay_req_ptr)->sequence_id !=
+			    hdr->sequence_id) {
+			break;
+		}
+
 		pdelay_req_state->rcvd_pdelay_follow_up++;
 
 		/* If we already have one, drop the received one. */
-- 
2.43.0

